  "question": {
    "text": "For an ideal gas the instantaneous change in pressure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> with volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> is given by the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>.</mo></math> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> is the given boundary condition, then the maximum temperature one mole of gas can attain is:&nbsp;(Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is the gas constant)"
  },
